L000482	\subsection{Compressibility and Magnetic Susceptibility}
L000483	Thermodynamic quantities can also be extracted from Eq.~\eqref{IoffeLarkin}, but now in the $\omega=0$, $q\rightarrow 0$ limit. The polarization of the $f$ parton Fermi liquid is $\Pi_{f}=\begin{bmatrix}
L000484	    \frac{m^*}{2\pi} & 0\\
L000485	    0 & -\frac{q^2}{12\pi m^*}
L000486	    \end{bmatrix}$. Together with the polarization tensor of $d_1$ with mass $m_1$, $\Pi_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}
L000487	    \frac{q^2}{12\pi \abs{m_1}} & \pm\frac{iq}{2\pi}\\
L000488	    \mp\frac{iq}{2\pi} & -\frac{q^2}{12\pi \abs{m_1}}
L000489	    \end{bmatrix}$ with the upper(lower) sign corresponding to the CFL(FL) phase, and $d_2$, which is assumed to contribute $\Pi_{2}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\begin{bmatrix}
L000490	    0 & iq\\
L000491	    -iq & 0
L000492	    \end{bmatrix}$ for simplicity, we find that the electron polarization tensor is given by
L000493	    \begin{equation}
L000494	    \begin{aligned}
L000495	        \Pi_e=&\frac{q^2}{\left(\frac{2\pi}{m^*}+\frac{\pi}{3\abs{m_1}}\right)12\pi m^*+\left(2\pi\pm2\pi\right)^2}\\
L000496	        &\times\begin{bmatrix}
L000497	    \frac{12\pi m^*}{q^2} & i\frac{2\pi\pm2\pi}{q}\\
L000498	    -i\frac{2\pi\pm2\pi}{q} & -\left(\frac{2\pi}{m^*}+\frac{\pi}{3\abs{m_1}}\right)
L000499	    \end{bmatrix}.
L000500	    \end{aligned}
L000501	    \end{equation}
L000502	From this, we extract the compressibility (magnetic susceptibility) through $\Pi_e^{00}$ ($\Pi_e^{yy}/q^2$).
L000503	
L000504	For the insulator* phase, the only difference is that $d_1$ parton does not have a Hall response and $\Pi_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}
L000505	    \frac{q^2}{\Delta_1} & 0\\
L000506	    0 & -\frac{q^2}{\Delta_1}
L000507	    \end{bmatrix}$ with $\Delta_1$ the gap scale of the $d_1$ parton and $\Delta_1\approx 12\pi \min(\abs{m_1'},\abs{m_1''})$. This, in turn, gives an incompressible state with
L000508	    \begin{equation}
L000509	        \Pi_e=\begin{bmatrix}
L000510	    \frac{q^2}{\Delta_1} & 0\\
L000511	    0 & -\frac{q^2}{12\pi m^* +\Delta_1}
L000512	    \end{bmatrix}
L000513	    \end{equation}
L000514	which has the same form as a band insulator, but we note that the relation between electric polarizability and magnetic susceptibility is non-trivial. As one approaches the phase transition, $\Delta_1\rightarrow 0$, indicating a diverging electric polarizability, while the magnetic susceptibility saturates due to the $f$ parton Fermi surface, in contrast to a conventional band insulator.
