L000589	\textit{Composite fermion theory} - The low-energy effective theory of the CFL~\cite{halperin_theory_1993} is described by a Fermi liquid phase of composite fermions (CF), which are electrons attached by two flux quanta. This composition of an electron and two flux quanta is realized by a $U(1)$ gauge field $a$~\cite{lopez_fractional_1991,halperin_theory_1993}. The effective Lagrangian reads
L000590	\begin{equation}
L000591	    \mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_\text{FL}[f^\dagger,f,A-a]+\frac{1}{2}\frac{1}{4\pi}a d a,
L000592	\end{equation}
L000593	where $f^\dagger$ ($f$) is the creation (annihilation) operator of a composite fermion, $ada=\epsilon_{\mu\lambda\nu}a_\mu \partial_\lambda a_\nu$ is the Chern-Simons term. $\mathcal{L}_\text{FL}[f^\dagger,f, A-a]$ describes the Lagrangian of a Fermi liquid of composite fermions in a total gauge field $A-a$, with $A$ the background gauge field that we assume to be static throughout this paper. Since the Lagrangian is non-relativistic, the time component of the dynamical $U(1)$ gauge field $a$ acts as a Lagrange multiplier. Integating out $a_0$, we find the flux attachment condition $4\pi f^\dagger(r) f(r)= \nabla \times \Vec{a}(r)$, where $r$ labels the position. Under the Random Phase Approximation (RPA), the electron polarization tensor $\Pi_e$, which differs from the electron response function by a minus sign, is given by~\cite{ioffe_gapless_1989}
L000594	\begin{equation}
L000595	    \Pi_e^{-1}=\Pi_f^{-1}+\Pi_\text{\tiny CS}^{-1}
L000596	\end{equation}
L000597	with $\Pi_f$ the one-loop polarization bubble of CF, and $\Pi_\text{\tiny CS}=\frac{1}{8\pi}\begin{bmatrix}
L000598	    0 & iq\\
L000599	    -iq & 0
L000600	\end{bmatrix}$ is the polarization tensor of the flux quanta in Coulomb gauge, with wave vector $\Vec{q}=(q,0)$. The first entry of the matrix is to be understood as the time component, and the second one is the $\hat{y}$ component. In the long-wavelength, high-frequency limit, we have $\Pi_f=-\frac{\mathcal{D}}{2}\begin{bmatrix}
L000601	    \frac{q^2}{\omega^2} & 0\\
L000602	    0 & 1
L000603	\end{bmatrix}$. The connection between the conductivity tensor and the polarization tensor is $\sigma=2T \Pi T$ with $T=\begin{bmatrix}
L000604	    \sqrt{\frac{\omega}{iq^2}} & 0\\
L000605	    0 & \sqrt{\frac{-i}{\omega}}
L000606	\end{bmatrix}$, from which we obtain the summation rule Eq.~\eqref{ILRule}.
