L000627	\textit{Parton description} - The phase transition between CFL and FL~\cite{barkeshli_continuous_2012,song_phase_2024} is proposed to be described by parton theory~\cite{jain_incompressible_1989,wen_non-abelian_1991,wen_projective_1999,wen_quantum_2004}. In the parton theory, the original electron is fractionalized into three fermionic partons, $c^\dagger=f^\dagger d_1^\dagger d_2^\dagger$, with $f^\dagger$ ($f$) and $d_i^\dagger$ ($d_i$) the creation (annihilation) operators of fermionic partons which \textit{mutually commute}.
L000628	Under the assumption that the partons are the proper degrees of freedom to describe the state, as a low-energy theory one considers nearly free partons subjected to dynamical gauge fields. The gauge structure between free partons is necessary to impose the constraint that the only physical degrees of freedom are electrons. In terms of parton operators, this constraint corresponds to $f^\dagger f (r)= d_1^\dagger d_1 (r)=d_2^\dagger d_2(r)$.\footnote{The full gauge group is $SU(3)$. Here, we assume other components of $SU(3)$ except for two $U(1)$ are broken.} In CFL, it is assumed that $f$ forms a Fermi liquid state and $d_1$, $d_2$ are Chern insulators with number $\mathcal{C}=1$. Integrating out $d_1$ and $d_2$ gives the CS term $\Pi_\text{\tiny CS}$ in the long wavelength and small frequency limit; and we get back to the CFL effective Lagrangian. The transition to FL is described by a TPT of one of the $d_i$ partons, say $d_1$, from $\mathcal{C}=1$ to $\mathcal{C}=-1$ while the other two partons do not undergo phase transitions. This TPT of parton changes the CS polarization to a Maxwell-like polarization and serves the role of Fermi liquid parameters. More explicitly, we have
L000629	\begin{equation}
L000630	    \mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_\text{FL}[f^\dagger,f,A-a-b]+\mathcal{L}_1[d_1^\dagger,d_1,a]+\mathcal{L}_2[d_2^\dagger,d_2,b]
L000631	\end{equation}
L000632	with $\mathcal{L}_1$ ($\mathcal{L}_2$) the effective Lagrangian for $d_1$ ($d_2$). We will consider $\mathcal{L}_\text{FL}[f^\dagger,f, A-a-b]$ to be unchanged across the phase transition and $\mathcal{L}_2$ to be a $\mathcal{C}=1$ insulator with an infinite gap and infinitesimal length scale (which is a good assumption as long as the parton gap is larger than the plasmon gap). Under this assumption, integrating out $d_2$ gives a pure CS term $\frac{1}{4\pi}bdb$. The TPT from $\mathcal{C}=1$ to $\mathcal{C}=-1$ is described by two 2-component Dirac fermions flipping the mass sign,
L000633	\begin{equation}
L000634	    \mathcal{L}_1=\sum_{i=1}^{2}\bar{\psi}_i  (i\slashed{\mathcal{D}}_{a}-m) \psi_i
L000635	    \label{diracf}
L000636	\end{equation}
L000637	where $\psi_i$ should be understood as the low-energy description of $d_1$ at different momenta that are effectively decoupled. $\slashed{\mathcal{D}}_a=\slashed{\partial}+\slashed{a}$ and the slashed operator $\slashed{\partial}=\partial_\mu \gamma^\mu$. $\eta_{\mu\nu}=\text{diag}(1,-1,-1)$, $\gamma^0=\sigma^3, \gamma^1=i \sigma^1, \gamma^2=i \sigma^2$ with $\sigma$ the Pauli matrices and $\bar{\psi}=\psi^\dagger \gamma^0$. At one-loop level, integrating out $d_1$ ($\psi_1$ and $\psi_2$) gives an effective action $\mathcal{L}_1=a\Pi_1 a=a_\mu\Pi_{\mu\nu}^\text{\tiny MW} a_\nu+a_\mu\Pi_{\mu\nu}^\text{\tiny CS} a_\nu$, composed of a Maxwell (MW) and a Chern-Simons (CS) contribution, where
L000638	\begin{widetext}
L000639	\begin{equation}
L000640	\begin{aligned}
L000641	    \Pi_{\mu\nu}^\text{\tiny MW}&=\frac{1}{4\pi}\left(-\eta_{\mu\nu}q_\alpha q^\alpha+q_\mu q_\nu\right) \frac{1}{q_E^3}\left(2q_E\abs{m}-(4m^2-q_E^2) \arctan(\frac{q_E}{2\abs{m}})\right)\\
L000642	    \Pi_{\mu\nu}^\text{\tiny CS}&=-\frac{im}{4\pi}\epsilon_{\mu\lambda\nu}q^\lambda \frac{2}{q_E}\arctan(\frac{q_E}{2\abs{m}})
L000643	\end{aligned}
L000644	\end{equation}
L000645	\end{widetext}
L000646	with $q_0=\omega$ and $q_E=\sqrt{-\omega^2+\abs{\vec{q}}^2}$. In the low-frequency and long-wavelength limit, $m^2\gg \abs{\vec{q}}^2+\omega^2$, the polarization reduces to a pure CS term, $\frac{\mathcal{C}}{4\pi}ada$ with $\mathcal{C}=-1$ ($\mathcal{C}=1$) for $m>0$ ($m<0$). For $m=0$, we get only the Maxwell term $\frac{1}{16}\left(-\eta_{\mu\nu}q_\alpha q^\alpha+q_\mu q_\nu\right)\frac{1}{q_E}$.
L000647	In the long-wavelength limit, $|\vec{q}|\rightarrow 0$, we set $\omega\rightarrow \omega+i\delta$ with $\delta$ infinitesimal to get the retarded function $\Pi_{00}=\frac{1}{16}\frac{\abs{\vec{q}}^2}{-i \omega}$. This gives the well-known constant optical conductivity of two massless Dirac fermions via $\sigma_{xx}(\omega)=\frac{2\omega}{i\abs{\vec{q}}^2}\Pi_{00}=\frac{1}{8}$.
