L000657	\textit{Plasmon} - To study the plasmon mode, we first take the long-wavelength and finite frequency limit of $\Pi_1$. An important property of the polarization tensor $\Pi_1$ is that when $\omega>2\abs{m}$, an imaginary part appears. Physically, this corresponds to the particle-hole continuum of the Dirac fermions. The polarization of the electron (corresponding to the response to the background gauge field $A$) is obtained by integrating out all fermionic partons and dynamical gauge fields $a$ and $b$, at the RPA level,
L000658	\begin{equation}
L000659	    \Pi_e^{-1}=\Pi_f^{-1}+\Pi_{1}^{-1}+\Pi_{2}^{-1}.
L000660	\end{equation}
L000661	In Fig.~\ref{plasmon_cfl}, we plot the imaginary part of $\frac{\Pi_{e,00}}{q^2}$, which reflects the eigenmodes of the electron density-density response function. On the CFL side ($m<0$), when $\abs{m}\gg\omega_p$, we get a plasmon mode with frequency $\omega\approx \omega_p=4\pi \mathcal{D}$. As one gets closer to the deconfined TPT ($m=0$), the frequency of the plasmon gets smaller because the CS term from Dirac fermions becomes smaller when the frequency is comparable to the mass of the Dirac fermions. Near the TPT, the plasmon gets damped when it gets into the particle-hole continuum of the Dirac fermions, as one can see in Fig.~\ref{plasmon_cfl}, the long lifetime plasmon gets damped quickly after entering the fan of the particle-hole continuum set by $\omega> 2\abs{m}$. The frequency of the sharp plasmon mode could be understood as a zero point in the determinant of the inverse of electron polarization, $\det[\Pi_e^{-1}](\omega)=0$, as a function of frequency. In the presence of the particle-hole continuum, we cannot find a real solution of $\det[\Pi_e^{-1}](\omega)=0$. Instead, a finite imaginary part of $\Pi_1$ arises, which effectively shifts the real solution of $\det[\Pi_e^{-1}](\omega)=0$ at large $\abs{m}$ to a complex one near the TPT and damps the plasmon mode. The dynamical gauge field is important for this damping phenomenon since it dictates the relation $\Pi_e^{-1}=\Pi_f^{-1}+\Pi_{1}^{-1}+\Pi_{2}^{-1}$ at the RPA level and, together with the particle-hole continuum, shifts the solution of $\det[\Pi_e^{-1}](\omega)=0$ to a complex value. This is very similar to Landau damping in a FL, where the plasmon gets damped when it enters the particle-hole continuum above a finite wave vector; the difference here is that this damping happens at any wave vector near the phase transition, also in the long-wavelength limit.
L000662	
L000663	At the critical point ($m=0$), we have
L000664	$\Pi_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}
L000665	    \frac{i q^2}{16\omega} & 0\\
L000666	    0& \frac{i \omega^2}{16\omega}
L000667	    \end{bmatrix}$ in the Coulomb gauge, and consequently
L000668	\begin{equation}
L000669	\Pi_e=\frac{1}{(\frac{\omega}{D}+16i)^2-16\pi^2}\begin{bmatrix}
L000670	    -(\frac{1}{D}+\frac{16i}{\omega})q^2 & -4\pi iq\\
L000671	    4\pi iq & -(\frac{\omega^2}{D}+16i\omega)
L000672	    \end{bmatrix}.
L000673	\end{equation}
L000674	The imaginary part of $\Pi_e^{00}$ at small $\omega$ is therefore
L000675	\begin{equation}
L000676	    \lim_{\omega\rightarrow 0}\Im[\Pi_e^{00}]=\frac{1}{16+\pi^2}\frac{q^2}{\omega}
L000677	\end{equation}
L000678	which agrees with the linecuts shown in Fig.~\ref{plasmon_cfl}(b).  Surprisingly on the first sight, the conductivity at the critical point satisfies a "Galilean" f-sum rule, $\lim_{q\rightarrow 0}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}d\omega \omega \Im[\Pi_{e}^{00}(\omega)]=\pi q^2 D$, even though we use a relativistic Dirac fermion polarization tensor in composing the electron response function. However, this should not be understood as the familiar f-sum rule where the integral gives $\pi q^2 \frac{n}{m_e}$ with $m_e$ the bare mass of electrons, because the $D$ we used for the $f$ parton Fermi liquid depends on the interacting energy scale, and we do not have Galilean invariance to begin with. We note that we use free Dirac fermions as the critical theory, which do not faithfully reflect the critical behavior. However, as we focus on the qualitative behavior, we expect the general features to still persist with a more accurate critical theory. Remarkably, the mechanism for plasmon damping is insensitive to the precise critical exponents. We expect this damping to happen even when the transition is weakly first-order, since the plasmon damping arises in a finite range around the critical point.
