L000694	\textit{Conductivity} - Using our formalism, we now determine the conductivity near the phase transition. One of the unique properties of the CFL phase is the wave vector-dependent conductivity $\Re\sigma_{xx}\propto q$ for $\frac{4\pi }{k_F m^*}q^3<\omega< \frac{k_F}{m^*} q$ with $k_F$ the Fermi momentum, $m^*$ the effective mass, and $n$ the density of the $f$ parton. In contrast, the characteristic quantity of the FL is the Drude weight, which leads to $\sigma_{xx}\propto -\frac{D}{i\omega}$ in the absence of impurity scattering. We start from the FL ($m>0$) side, in the low frequency limit, $m\gg \omega\gg q$, we have $\Pi_{1}=\frac{1}{12\pi \abs{m}}\begin{bmatrix}
L000695	    q^2 & 0\\
L000696	    0& \omega^2
L000697	    \end{bmatrix}+\frac{1}{4\pi}\begin{bmatrix}
L000698	    0 & -iq\\
L000699	    iq& 0
L000700	    \end{bmatrix}.$
L000701	This leads to an electron polarization
L000702	\begin{equation}
L000703	    \Pi_{e}=-\frac{1}{\frac{1}{\mathcal{D}}+\frac{4\pi}{3m}}\begin{bmatrix}
L000704	    \frac{q^2}{\omega^2} & 0\\
L000705	    0 & 1
L000706	    \end{bmatrix}
L000707	\end{equation}
L000708	which describes an FL with Drude weight $\frac{1}{\frac{1}{D}+\frac{4\pi}{3m}}$. We find the Drude weight saturates at $D$ for large $m$ and decreases continuously to zero as $m\rightarrow 0$. Again, the linear decrease of Drude weight at small $m$ is a result of a free Dirac fermion, which could be changed by the critical theory. To extract the linear in $q$ conductivity in the CFL ($m<0$) phase, we need the polarization tensors for $\frac{k_F}{nm^*}q^3<\omega< \frac{k_F}{m^*} q$. In this limit, the $f$ Fermi liquid contributes $\Pi_{f}\approx\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}
L000709	    \frac{m^*}{2\pi} & 0\\
L000710	    0& \frac{2in\omega}{k_F q}
L000711	    \end{bmatrix}$.
L000712	When $q\ll \abs{m}$, the $d_1$ Dirac fermions contribute $\Pi_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}
L000713	    \frac{q^2}{24\pi \abs{m}} & \frac{iq}{4\pi}\\
L000714	    -\frac{iq}{4\pi} & -\frac{q^2}{24\pi \abs{m}}
L000715	    \end{bmatrix}$ and we arrive at the linear in $q$ conductivity $\sigma_{xx}(q,\omega)=\frac{q}{32\pi k_F}$, while for $q\gg \abs{m}$, $\Pi_{1}=\begin{bmatrix}
L000716	    \frac{q}{16} & -\frac{m}{4}\\
L000717	    -\frac{m}{4} & -\frac{q}{16}
L000718	    \end{bmatrix}$, which gives us $\sigma_{xx}(q,\omega)=\frac{\frac{16i\omega}{q}-\frac{k_F q}{2n}}{(16+\frac{2\pi}{m^*})(-16+\frac{k_F q^2}{2in\omega})-16\pi^2}$ with a complicate $(q,\omega)$ dependence. Thus, the linear in $q$ conductivity $\sigma_{xx}$ terminates at around scale $q_c\sim \abs{m}$ as shown in Fig.~\ref{cond_cfl}. In the simulation, we set the $f$ parton Fermi liquid to have an effective mass $m^*=1$ and Fermi wavevector $k_F=\sqrt{4\pi}$ so that the Drude weight of the $f$ parton Fermi liquid is $D=\frac{k_F^2}{4\pi}=1$. Starting from a large Dirac fermion mass $m$, when $\frac{k_F q}{m^*} >\omega$, we get a real part of conductivity from the particle-hole continuum of the $f$ parton Fermi liquid and when $\frac{4\pi q^3}{k_F m^*}\ll \omega$, $\Re\sigma_{xx}\propto q$. When the gap of the Dirac fermion, $2m$, gets below $\omega$, we get additional real conductivity at small $q$ from the dissipation channels of the Dirac fermions.
